<header><p class="st-kicker">other · Korea and Japan</p><h1 class="st-title">Tottori Domain</h1><p class="st-meta">1600 CE – 1871 CE · capital Tottori</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The domain of Inaba and Hoki on the Sea of Japan coast, ruled for most of the Edo period by a senior branch of the Ikeda family.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tottori">Tottori</a> castle had been the scene of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/toyotomi-hideyoshi">Toyotomi Hideyoshi's</a> notorious starvation siege of 1581. After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-sekigahara">Sekigahara</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ikeda-nagayoshi">Ikeda Nagayoshi</a> held Tottori with a small fief. In 1617 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shogun">shogunate</a> gave <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ikeda-mitsumasa">Ikeda Mitsumasa</a> the provinces of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/inaba-province">Inaba</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hoki-province">Hoki</a>, with 325,000 koku.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>In 1632 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ikeda-mitsumasa">Mitsumasa</a> exchanged domains with his cousin <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ikeda-mitsunaka">Ikeda Mitsunaka</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/okayama">Okayama</a>, and Mitsunaka's line ruled <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tottori">Tottori</a> until 1871. The domain governed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hoki-province">Hoki</a> through a senior retainer family stationed at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yonago">Yonago</a> castle. Its economy rested on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rice">rice</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cotton">cotton</a> and iron sand smelting in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chugoku-region">Chugoku</a> mountains, and it guarded the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sea-of-japan">Sea of Japan</a> coast.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>The last lord, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ikeda-yoshinori">Ikeda Yoshinori</a>, a brother of the last <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shogun">shogun</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tokugawa-yoshinobu">Tokugawa Yoshinobu</a>, nevertheless sided with the imperial forces in 1868. After abolition in 1871 the domain became <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tottori-prefecture">Tottori Prefecture</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/tottori-domain.jpg" alt="Tottori Domain" width="320" height="456"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ikeda_Yoshinori.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1600 CE – 1871 CE</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>other</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Tottori</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Ikeda Mitsumasa, Ikeda Mitsunaka, Ikeda Yoshinori</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Buddhism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Japanese</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/japan">Japan</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Inaba Domain</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1736&amp;focus=tottori-domain" aria-label="Tottori Domain on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="8073 895 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Tottori Domain lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M8393 1151L8530 1145M8393 1151L8529 1145"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M8530 1145h0M8529 1145h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M8393 1151h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M8393 1151h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Tottori. <a href="/atlas?year=1736&amp;focus=tottori-domain">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
